Concern: the issue record names a narrowing view but not where its values are
Upstream: not-submitted

An issue operand carries the declared shape, the row window and a byte count that is zero when the
operand is not one span (0101). A kernel that receives a strided tensor -- the GDN conv prep reads
its gate and beta as two four-column views of the fused 136-wide input, and the q norm reads the
query half of an interleaved q|gate buffer -- records the declared buffer and no offset. A
consumer that must copy those values (the training tape keeps them for the backward) cannot tell
which elements the kernel saw: it has the row window and nothing else. This adds the tensor the
kernel actually receives as its own rank, shape, strides and element offset from the declared
buffer, for the strided case only, so a contiguous operand still reads as one span.

--- a/abi/rad_runtime.h
+++ b/abi/rad_runtime.h
@@ -934,6 +934,14 @@
     int64_t  shape[RAD_MAX_RANK]; /* the declared shape, before this issue narrows dim 0 */
     int64_t  offset;       /* element offset into the buffer or weight, as issued */
     int64_t  rows;         /* dim 0 extent this issue uses; 0 means the declared extent */
+    /* The tensor the kernel receives, when it is not the declared one: a view narrows dim 0, or
+     * strides the last axis (the q norm reads the query half of the interleaved q|gate buffer).
+     * `view_off` is its first element's offset from the declared buffer's, and `view_stride` the
+     * strides into the declared layout. `bytes` is 0 for such a view: it is not one span. */
+    uint8_t  view_rank;
+    int64_t  view_shape[RAD_MAX_RANK];
+    int64_t  view_stride[RAD_MAX_RANK];
+    int64_t  view_off;
     uint8_t  written;      /* 1 for RAD_OPD_OUT and RAD_OPD_INOUT, 0 otherwise */
     uint8_t  inout;        /* 1 for RAD_OPD_INOUT, whose previous value the kernel also reads */
 } RadIssueOperand;

--- a/core/runtime/issue.cpp
+++ b/core/runtime/issue.cpp
@@ -1365,6 +1365,19 @@
         }
         /* A KV operand's offset is its layer, not an element offset. */
         if (o.kind == RAD_OPK_KV) e.offset = 0;
+        /* The view the kernel actually receives, when it is not the declared tensor; a consumer
+         * that has to copy the operand's values cannot read them as one span. */
+        if (full && o.kind == RAD_OPK_BUF && full->rank > 0 &&
+            !rad_tensor_is_contiguous(&t[i])) {
+            e.view_rank = (uint8_t)t[i].rank;
+            for (uint32_t d = 0; d < t[i].rank && d < RAD_MAX_RANK; ++d) {
+                e.view_shape[d]  = t[i].shape[d];
+                e.view_stride[d] = t[i].stride[d];
+            }
+            const int64_t es = rad_dtype_bytes(t[i].dtype, 1);
+            if (es > 0 && rad_dtype_bits(t[i].dtype) % 8 == 0 && t[i].data && full->data)
+                e.view_off = ((const uint8_t*)t[i].data - (const uint8_t*)full->data) / es;
+        }
         if (o.kind != RAD_OPK_WTAB && rad_tensor_is_contiguous(&t[i]))
             e.bytes = rad_dtype_bytes(t[i].dtype, rad_tensor_numel(&t[i]));
         const int role = (p.info && p.info->schema && i < p.info->schema->n_operands)
